# End-to-end EMULATOR test for the BIGTREETECH SKR V1.4 (NXP LPC1768)
# in DETERMINISTIC TICK MODE. Boots klipper firmware under Renode
# against the skr-v1.4 pin map, completes klippy identify + clocksync,
# brings up the full MCU config (4 TMC2208s, 2 software-PWM heaters, a
# fan, endstops, 2 thermistors), reads the thermistors through the
# IRQ-driven LPC ADC, round-trips the four TMC2208 register files over
# their per-driver software UART, and exercises interactive gcode
# (M114/M105) against real firmware.
#
# This is the LPC176x per-printer test, the sibling of skr_pico
# (RP2040) and skr_mini_e3_v2 (STM32F103). What it adds over those:
#
#   - SINGLE-WIRE-PER-DRIVER TMC UART. The four TMC2208s each have their
#     own dedicated uart_pin (P1.10/P1.9/P1.8/P1.4, rx == tx, no tx_pin,
#     no multi-drop address) instead of sharing one bus by datagram
#     address. The responder can't tell from a TMC2208 request which pin
#     it targets, so renode_hooks.sw_uart broadcasts each framed reply
#     onto every registered RX pin; klippy initialises and reads the
#     drivers strictly sequentially from the shared register file, so the
#     content lines up and per-driver IFCNT write-verify passes. DUMP_TMC
#     below reads back the live register file for stepper_x (first pin)
#     and extruder (last pin), proving the per-driver UART read path at
#     both ends.
#
#   - The IRQ-DRIVEN LPC ADC. klipper's src/lpc176x/adc.c collects burst
#     samples in ADC_IRQHandler, so unlike the polled ADC stubs the other
#     chips use, nothing completes a thermistor read unless the ADC
#     interrupt actually fires. test/emulator/repl/lpc176x_adc.cs is a C#
#     model that raises ADC_IRQn (-> nvic@22) per conversion. With a
#     broken IRQ path the burst never finishes, klippy never receives an
#     analog_in_state, and the heaters silently sit at temp=0.0 with no
#     shutdown - so the "temp=3" assertion below (both EPCOS 100K
#     thermistors read ~36 C) is the positive proof the IRQ-driven ADC
#     delivered samples.
#
# Two LPC-specific harness pieces this exercises:
#   - scripts/test_klippy.py emits sw_uart for the LPC "Pn.m" pin names
#     (the 'LPCn' port sentinel); the STM32/SAM path only handled "PXn"
#     and RP2040 "gpioN".
#   - renode_hooks.sw_uart drives the firmware-side RX bit by writing the
#     LPC fast-GPIO FIOPIN register (0x2009C000 + port*0x20 + 0x14),
#     because the LPC GPIO is modelled as a plain storeback region.
#
# The real SKR V1.4 is USB-CDC / UART0; the firmware here is the generic
# serial-mode lpc176x build (lpc176x.dict, host link on P0.3/P0.2) and
# the test exercises the pin map, which is independent of the host link.
# As with skr_pico / skr_mini_e3_v2, the gated path is motion-free.
DICTIONARY lpc176x.dict
CONFIG skr_v1_4.cfg
EMULATOR skr_v1_4.fixture.json
REQUIRES_EMULATOR  # deterministic-time lockstep needs the real backend

# Interactive gcode round-trip against real firmware in tick mode.
M114
M105
# Read back the live TMC register file over the per-driver UART for the
# first and last drivers. DUMP_TMC reads every register (incl. IOIN)
# live, so it only produces a full dump if the firmware round-tripped the
# read datagrams for that driver's pin.
DUMP_TMC STEPPER=stepper_x
DUMP_TMC STEPPER=extruder

# klippy connected, loaded the dictionary, and built the full config
# against real firmware.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Loaded MCU 'mcu'"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "Configured MCU 'mcu'"
# DUMP_TMC dumped the first and last per-driver pins; the IOIN read
# returning the TMC version byte (0x21) proves the single-wire UART read
# path returned framed, CRC-valid responses on each dedicated pin.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "DUMP_TMC stepper_x"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "DUMP_TMC extruder"
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "version=0x21"
# Positive proof the IRQ-driven LPC ADC delivered burst samples: both
# thermistors read ~36 C, so a periodic Stats line carries "temp=36.x".
# A broken ADC IRQ leaves them at temp=0.0 with no shutdown, which this
# catches where the no-error assertions below cannot.
EXPECT_LOG_CONTAINS "temp=3"
# A broken UART round-trip surfaces as a TMC init failure or a direct
# read/write error; the deterministic tick clock must produce neither.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "failed to init"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Unable to read tmc uart"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Unable to write tmc uart"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Internal error"
# A renode-side crash (wrong sysbus peripheral, hook callback error)
# kills serial; klippy then gets EOF and TMC init / identify time out.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Got EOF when reading from device"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "failed to init: Timeout"
# With the deterministic clock the config / TMC / ADC path pushes no
# deadline into the firmware's past, so nothing shuts down.
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "ADC out of range"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Timer too close"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Rescheduled timer in the past"
EXPECT_LOG_NOT_CONTAINS "Klippy is shutdown"
